---
import Layout from '../layouts/Layout.astro';
import Header from '../components/Header.astro';
import Footer from '../components/Footer.astro';
---

<Layout title="About — PCT Sierra">
  <Header />

  <main class="pt-20 pb-16 px-4 sm:px-6 min-h-screen">
    <div class="max-w-2xl mx-auto py-12">

      <p class="text-amber-600 text-sm font-medium tracking-widest uppercase mb-3">About</p>
      <h1 class="text-4xl font-bold text-stone-900 mb-8" style="font-family: 'Lora', serif;">
        You Don't Have to Disappear
      </h1>

      <div class="space-y-6 text-stone-600 text-lg leading-relaxed">
        <p>
          My entire adult life, I've watched the same reel play out on social media: someone quits
          their job, disappears into the wilderness for six months, and comes back with a beard and
          a book deal. I get it. There's something deeply romantic about the all-in thru-hike. But
          it was never going to be me.
        </p>

        <p>
          Not because I couldn't do it — but because I didn't want to put my life on pause to prove
          something to myself. And honestly? I wasn't sure I'd come out the other side still loving
          the trail.
        </p>

        <p>
          So I asked a different question: <em>what if I didn't have to choose?</em>
        </p>

        <p>
          That question sent me on a multi-year experiment in compromise, logistics, and obsession.
          Out-and-backs. Connector miles. Predawn drives. Summers with no social life. There are real
          costs to this approach, and I won't pretend otherwise.
        </p>

        <p>
          But the rewards are something a traditional thru-hike can't touch. I've seen the Sierra in
          every season — spring snowpack, summer heat, the eerie quiet of an October that belongs
          entirely to you. I've watched the trail flood with northbound thru-hikers and I've had it
          completely to myself. I've explored the alternates and connector trails that thru-hikers
          blow past by design. I've brought friends. I've taken my time. And through all of it, I've
          stayed rooted — in my career, my relationships, my community.
        </p>

        <p>
          That last part matters more than people expect. You don't have to disappear to find
          something out there. You carry the trail back into the week with you. Monday morning hits
          differently when 24 hours ago you were watching the sun come up over a granite ridgeline.
        </p>

        <p>
          The PCTA issues roughly 7,000 thru and section hike permits each year. Hundreds of
          thousands more people visit that same trail. Thru-hikers get all the glory — and that's
          fine. They've earned it. But this site is for everyone else. The weekend warriors. The
          people who pack a week's worth of living into 48 hours and show up to work on Monday a
          little tired and completely alive.
        </p>

        <p>
          If you're wondering whether this kind of life is possible — this site is the answer.
        </p>
      </div>

    </div>
  </main>

  <Footer />
</Layout>
